+\paragraph{Prior work and the present contribution.}
+The use of parameter-dependent exponential periods and
+Siegel--Shidlovskii theory is established in the work of Fres\'an and
+Jossen.  Jossen's 2018 report develops polynomial-phase families with
+noncompact rapid-decay cycles and states the Andr\'e--Beukers lifting
+principle for polynomial relations \cite[\S\S2--3]{Jossen2018}.
+For compact loops on $\mathbb G_m$, Fres\'an and Jossen derive
+$E$-function systems from linearity, differentiation, and twisted Stokes
+\cite[\S12.7]{FresanJossenExp}.  They also identify the absolute
+polynomial-phase connection with the Fourier--Laplace transform of the
+reduced polynomial direct image \cite[Proposition~5.1]{FresanJossen}.
+
+In a 2025 lecture reporting joint work in progress with Fres\'an, Jossen
+presented the quadratic marked-point model, including
+$\int_0^a e^{-zx^2}\,dx$ at algebraic $a$ and the endpoint exponentials
+\cite{Jossen2025}.  He described its differential Galois group and explained
+the arithmetic independence obtained from Siegel--Shidlovskii theory,
+separating the additional $\sqrt\pi$-independence question for the full
+rapid-decay period field.  Our contribution is the uniform rigidity theorem
+for compact algebraic boundary data and arbitrary polynomial phase, together
+with the complete fixed-$q$ linear-relation criterion.  The argument uses
+constant-coordinate endomorphism rigidity and inverse-branch densities,
+without assuming simple critical points or distinct critical values.
+
